From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 15:44:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 37/52] fix(canvas): the GPU layer at any screen density, and
 shells where the GPU falls short

For the GPU picture layer (doop:gpu): it fixes what that commit left
untested (DPR > 1) and covers the paths it did not test (touch, remote
changes, marquee), before a render trial may turn the layer on for a
browser. Nothing changes where the layer is off.

Names at DPR > 1. Chromium shapes a 12px title at 12 * dpr device px, and
those hinted advances are not the 12px ones scaled, so a name measured at
12px was cut at another letter than its title and drifted by pixels. The
atlas now sets the font at its device size and divides the widths back
(gpuLabels.ts). Measured against the DOM title with
--force-device-scale-factor, 80 names: CSS-size measure off by a mean of
1.7 / 1.9 / 0.9 / 1.2 / 0.7 px (max 6.4 / 6 / 5.3 / 5 / 2.7) at 1.25 / 1.5 /
1.75 / 2 / 3; device-size measure 0 at all but 3 (mean 0.09, max 0.67).
Screenshots of the 188-frame page, DOM shells against drawn frames, 40 name
boxes: at 2x mean |diff| 12.3 -> 0.8 /255 with 25 -> 0 boxes shifted; at 3x
11.2 -> 0.5 with 26 -> 0; at 1.5x 0.7, none shifted. Pictures were already
in place (0 shifted at 1.5, 2, 3 now; mean 0.1-1.0 /255).

A backing store the GPU will not give. Chromium hands a WebGL canvas less
than asked past its limits, capped by area and not in proportion (9000x5400
asked came back 7094x4676 with SwiftShader), and the draw, laid out for the
size asked, put every picture in the wrong place. Sizing moves out of draw
into resize(): a size past MAX_VIEWPORT_DIMS, or a drawing buffer that is
not the size asked, drops the canvas to 1x1 and fitsStage goes false, so
StagePictures draws nothing and every frame is its shell until a resize
fits again. Context restore resizes before drawing.

A density change at run time. The resolution media query is still watched,
but under emulation it fired one change late, so devicePixelRatio is also
compared on every camera move and scene update (a read, no layout), and a
change re-measures the stage and rebuilds the names.

Rejected: drawing at the reduced density the GPU did give (the cap is not
proportional, so x and y would need their own scale, and the shaders and
the hit arithmetic take one u_dpr; shells are the safe fallback and the
case is rare: 1600x1000 CSS px needs past 5x to reach 8192); measuring at
12px and scaling the drawn glyphs (the advances are what differ, not the
glyph size).

Checked in Chromium with SwiftShader, drawn frames against shells: double
click, shift click, Hand and middle drag, Comment click, alt drag,
Prototype click, right click (menu), marquee and shift marquee;
a phone at 390x844 (tap selects, a hold opens the menu only after
TOUCH_HOLD_MS, one finger pans from a drawn frame in the Hand and Comment
tools); a touch laptop (tap, drag, pinch); a peer's drag, rename, hide,
restack, delete, create and HTML edit, an agent's stream; panning at 60%
and 20% with shell culling: 0 frames neither drawn nor shelled, 0 both.

Tests (gpuPicturesBrowser.test.ts): marquee and shift press; a peer's
move, hide, delete, create and an agent's stream; a density change to 2x
and past the GPU's limit (every frame a shell, the canvas 1 px) and back;
names measured at 1.25x, 1.5x and 2x (a guard: setViewport's emulated
density does not shape text at the device size, so it cannot fail the old
measure); a phone: pan in the Hand and Comment tools, tap, long hold.
Pixel reads stay within the viewport: a capture beyond it rendered white
where the screen shows paper.

Camera cost unchanged, 188-frame page at fit, SwiftShader, wheel pan,
off / force interleaved, two rounds (load 8-11): layers 9 with doop:gpu
forced (as before); main thread 274-329 -> 115-173 ms, Layerize 140-158
-> 10-17 ms, compositor 182-240 -> 87-113 ms.

